  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe upper-level ridge axis will migrate westward from the Tennessee \nValley to the Central Plains over the next 24 hours, placing our \nforecast region in the favorable zone for thunderstorms over the \nnext two days on the periphery of the ridge. In addition, a cold \nfront will slowly approach from the north today, sweeping through \nthe forecast area Saturday. \n\nThe combination of favorable instability (1,000 to 2,000 J/kg of \nMLCAPE) and modest 0-6 km bulk shear (20-35 kts) will create support \nfor strong to severe thunderstorm development this afternoon. The \nstrongest shear will be located across northern West Virginia and \nsoutheast Ohio today. Today's severe threats will mainly be damaging \nwind gusts and some isolated large hail.\n\nSaturday's dynamics will be very similar to today's with favorable \ninstability and shear. However, the main focus for severe \nthunderstorms will likely be across southern zones, ahead of the \ncold front. The Storm Prediction Center has a slight risk of severe \nweather outlooked Saturday across southwest Virginia and portions of \nsouthern West Virginia and northeast Kentucky. \n\nHigh pressure will build back into the region from the north Sunday, \nlargely ending thunderstorm chances for most areas until mid to late \nnext week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nExcessive heat and humidity continues today with high temperatures \nexpected to reach the middle and upper 90s across the lowlands. Heat \nindex values will once again reach the lower 100s across these \nareas. Be sure to practice heat safety in these conditions. Take \nbreaks in the air conditioning, drink plenty of water and \nelectrolytes, and frequently check on neighbors without air \nconditioning.\n\nSaturday will be hot, but temperatures and heat index values will \nlikely be just below advisory criteria with scattered to widespread \nclouds and convection ahead of a cold front. Some relief from the \nheat and humidity will arrive by Sunday. Temperatures will top out \nin the lower to middle 80s for highs, and dew points will also run a \nlittle lower, topping out in the upper 60s to lower 70s instead of \nthe lower to middle 70s.",
